The next piece is **Axis 2 Slice 1: stale bridge evidence**.

Not another budget case. Budget already proves the core composition failure: two branch-local bridges can be honest and still fail globally at merge. The next distinct failure mode should stress **time / horizon / premise freshness**.

## Axis 2 Slice 1 — Stale Evidence Merge

Keeper line:

> Local preservation evidence is not compositional when its validity horizon expires before reconciliation.

Shape:

```text
Branch A: bridged under evidence horizon H₁
Branch B: bridged under evidence horizon H₂
Merge occurs at time T
One bridge was valid locally, but stale at merge time
Merged path cannot inherit both witnesses
```

This is different from budget:

* **BudgetMerge:** branch-local scope cannot see joint usage.
* **StaleEvidenceMerge:** branch-local evidence cannot survive time/premise drift.

That gives Axis 2 two genuinely different bad cases.

## Minimal model

Use a state with:

```lean
structure TimedState where
  valueOk : Bool
  evidenceExpiresAt : Nat
  now : Nat
```

Value stays viability-shaped:

```lean
value s := if s.valueOk then 1 else 0
```

Bridge requires:

```lean
bridge s act :=
  s.now ≤ s.evidenceExpiresAt ∧ value s ≤ value (run s act)
```

Then merge:

```lean
merge a b :=
  { valueOk := a.valueOk && b.valueOk
    evidenceExpiresAt := min a.evidenceExpiresAt b.evidenceExpiresAt
    now := max a.now b.now }
```

Bad case:

```text
A locally bridged at now = 5, expires = 10
B locally bridged at now = 5, expires = 10
Merge happens at now = 12
Merged evidence horizon is expired
```

The failure is not that either branch locally lost value. It’s that the bridge evidence had a temporal scope and the merge boundary tried to reuse it outside that scope.

## The theorem to aim for

```lean
theorem locally_bridged_branches_can_merge_with_stale_evidence :
  ∃ s aEnd bEnd merged,
    BridgedTraj timedEnv s aEnd ∧
    BridgedTraj timedEnv s bEnd ∧
    value aEnd = value s ∧
    value bEnd = value s ∧
    evidenceFresh aEnd ∧
    evidenceFresh bEnd ∧
    merged = mergeAt 12 aEnd bEnd ∧
    ¬ evidenceFresh merged
```

Notice: don’t force the merged `value` to drop yet. The point of this slice is not value loss directly. It is **bridge non-portability** under temporal invalidation.

If you want a value drop too, add it as a second theorem:

```lean
theorem stale_evidence_can_hide_value_loss_at_merge :
  ...
```

But the first theorem should isolate the freshness failure.

## Why this is the right next slice

Because after BudgetMerge, `MergeAdmissible` cannot just mean:

> combined usage within global cap

It must be broader:

> branch-local bridge evidence remains valid at the reconciliation boundary.

So the eventual positive object starts needing at least two dimensions:

```text
MergeAdmissible =
  joint-resource condition
  + evidence-freshness condition
  + reconciliation-policy condition
```

Now we’re actually earning the predicate.

## Keep ConflictMerge demoted

Former conflict merge becomes Slice 2 or appendix corner:

> reconciliation policy itself can be value-destroying

Useful, but still degenerate. Don’t make it the second flagship.

So the sequence is:

```text
Axis 2 Slice 0: SharedBudgetMerge
  Local scope failure / joint margin bust

Axis 2 Slice 1: StaleEvidenceMerge
  Temporal scope failure / stale bridge evidence

Axis 2 Slice 2: ConflictMerge
  Reconciliation policy failure / merge operator destroys value
```

Then, and only then, define `MergeAdmissible`.

The next pain is **staleness**. Very on-brand. Annoyingly so.

